🔥

🟤 7. Provisioners (Use Carefully)
🧠 What is a Provisioner?

👉 A provisioner in Terraform is used to execute scripts or commands after a resource is created or before it is destroyed

👉 Provisioners are not used to create infrastructure directly
They are used for post-creation actions

🔹 Simple Understanding
Terraform creates resource -> provisioner runs command/script

Example:

Create EC2
Then run a script on EC2

🔥 Why Provisioners Exist?
Sometimes after infrastructure is created, you may need to:

Install software
Copy files
Run initialization commands

👉 Terraform provisioners help do this

⚠️ Important Note

Provisioners should be used only when necessary

HashiCorp recommends avoiding them when better alternatives exist, such as:

User data
Cloud-init
Configuration management tools
Image baking

🔧 Types of Provisioners
1. `local-exec`
Runs command on the machine where Terraform is executed

2. `remote-exec`
Runs command on the remote resource, like an EC2 instance

3. `file`
Copies files from local machine to remote resource

🔥 1. local-exec Provisioner

👉 Runs on your local system

Example:

resource "null_resource" "example" {
  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "echo Terraform execution completed"
  }
}

✅ Use cases:

Write logs
Call local scripts
Trigger notifications

🔥 2. remote-exec Provisioner

👉 Runs commands inside the created remote server

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "demo" {
  ami           = "ami-0f5ee92e2d63afc18"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  key_name      = "my-key"

  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    inline = [
      "sudo yum update -y",
      "sudo yum install -y nginx",
      "sudo systemctl start nginx"
    ]
  }

  connection {
    type        = "ssh"
    user        = "ec2-user"
    private_key = file("my-key.pem")
    host        = self.public_ip
  }
}

👉 Here Terraform connects through SSH and runs commands on EC2

🔥 3. file Provisioner

👉 Copies a file from local system to remote machine

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "demo" {
  ami           = "ami-0f5ee92e2d63afc18"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  provisioner "file" {
    source      = "app.conf"
    destination = "/tmp/app.conf"
  }

  connection {
    type        = "ssh"
    user        = "ec2-user"
    private_key = file("my-key.pem")
    host        = self.public_ip
  }
}

🔹 The `connection` Block

For `remote-exec` and `file`, Terraform needs connection details

Common values:

type = "ssh"
user = "ec2-user"
private_key = file("my-key.pem")
host = self.public_ip

👉 Without proper connection, provisioners fail

🔥 When Provisioners Run

By default:

After resource creation

You can also run during destroy:

provisioner "local-exec" {
  when    = destroy
  command = "echo Resource is being deleted"
}

⚠️ Risks of Provisioners
Provisioners can be fragile because:

Network may fail
SSH may fail
Scripts may fail
Terraform becomes less predictable

👉 That is why they are considered last-resort tools

🔥 Better Alternatives
Instead of provisioners, prefer:

EC2 user data
Cloud-init
Ansible
Packer images
Kubernetes manifests

✅ Example:
Instead of `remote-exec` to install nginx, use EC2 `user_data`

🧠 Interview Questions
❓ What is a provisioner in Terraform?

👉
"A provisioner is used to run scripts or commands on local or remote systems as part of resource creation or destruction."

❓ What is the difference between `local-exec` and `remote-exec`?

👉
`local-exec` runs on the local machine where Terraform is executed
`remote-exec` runs on the remote resource

❓ Why are provisioners not recommended?

👉 Because they are less predictable, harder to maintain, and better alternatives usually exist

❓ What is the use of the `file` provisioner?

👉 To copy files from local machine to remote resource

🔥 Real Interview Scenario
❓ How would you install software on an EC2 instance using Terraform?

👉
"It can be done using `remote-exec`, but in production I prefer `user_data` or configuration management tools because provisioners are a last resort."

🧠 Best Practices
Avoid provisioners in production when possible
Use `user_data` for EC2 bootstrap
Use provisioners only for small or unavoidable tasks
Keep scripts simple and retry-safe
Do not rely on provisioners for long-term configuration management

✅ Quick Summary
Provisioner = run command/script
`local-exec` -> local machine
`remote-exec` -> remote server
`file` -> copy files
Use carefully and prefer better alternatives

Reply "yes" -> next:
